A software factory: a service that takes a brief, spawns a sandboxed coding
agent per variant, lets it write an application, verifies the result, and
reports back.

Generating the application is the easy part. The moment it works you are
running untrusted code that a language model wrote, at scale, on hardware you
pay for. That is a sandboxing and resource-isolation problem before it is an AI
problem, and it maps almost line for line onto per-tenant isolation. If you
already have [a k3s platform with real tenancy](/blog/k3s-platform-kit/), you
have most of it; what you lack is a deadline and a guaranteed teardown, which
are the two the agents actually need.

::: note
**Part 7 of 7 of [Where to start with self-hosting](/blog/running-things-on-your-own-hardware/)**, a series about building a platform on your own hardware and then arguing about whether to.

Previous: [What a game world server actually needs](/blog/what-a-game-world-server-needs/)
:::

